30078125, -0.01233673095703125, -0.004058837890625, 0.020355224609375, 0.040557861328125, 0.0447998046875, 0.0228118896484375, 0.002384185791015625, 0.0181884765625, -0.001163482666015625, -0.03240966796875, -0.00057220458984375, 2.2470951080322266e-05, 0.01232147216796875, -0.01441192626953125, -0.0150299072265625, 0.0129852294921875, -0.025299072265625, -0.033416748046875, 0.00876617431640625, -0.006805419921875, -0.0014219284057617188, 0.006134033203125, -0.0194854736328125, -0.01947021484375, 0.01690673828125, -0.00786590576171875, -0.034820556640625, 0.01506805419921875, -0.00980377197265625, 0.021484375, -0.0018253326416015625, -0.039794921875, -0.020355224609375, -0.0084075927734375, -0.004688262939453125, -0.00894927978515625, 0.005123138427734375, 0.0157928466796875, -0.00836944580078125, 0.0186309814453125, -0.0236663818359375, -0.014251708984375, 0.029083251953125, -0.007068634033203125, 0.01197052001953125, 0.017730712890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits. GA offers a unified mathematical language that can represent geometric transformations and relationships more naturally than traditional vector or matrix approaches, potentially simplifying the analysis of multidimensional systems and providing new insights into their structure.\n\nA central mathematical tool in the study of dynamical systems is the Laplace transform, which converts ordinary differential equations (ODEs) from the time domain to the Laplace (frequency) domain, facilitating their solution and analysis. However, the standard Laplace transform is defined for real or complex-valued functions, and its extension to functions valued in more general algebras\u2014such as GA\u2014remains nontrivial. This extension is necessary to fully leverage the expressive power of GA in the analysis of systems whose states or signals are naturally represented as multivectors.\n\nPrevious works have considered extensions of the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras (see, for example, Hitzer & Sangwine, 2013; Brackx et al., 2001; De Bie et al., 2012; Ablamowicz & Fauser, 2004). These approaches typically focus on specific low-dimensional Clifford algebras or impose restrictions on the class of admissible functions and the nature of the Laplace parameter. For instance, Hitzer & Sangwine (2013) address quaternionic Laplace transforms but do not generalize to arbitrary geometric algebras, while Brackx et al. (2001) consider Clifford analysis but restrict the Laplace parameter to scalars and avoid the complications of non-commutative exponentials. To our knowledge, a general and explicit definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in arbitrary low-dimensional geometric algebras, with a careful treatment of the exponential map and parameter choices, has not been provided in the literature.\n\nIn this work, our main novel contribution is to introduce a rigorous and explicit definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions within the framework of geometric algebra \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), for signatures with \\(p+q \\leq 5\\). Specifically, for a multivector-valued function \\( f(t) \\) in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), we define its Laplace transform as\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real scalar parameter. The exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is interpreted as the standard scalar exponential, ensuring commutativity with all multivectors in the integrand. We emphasize that, in our approach, \\( s \\) is always taken to be a scalar to avoid the ambiguities and complications arising from non-commutative exponentials when \\( s \\) is a general GA element. In geometric algebras, the exponential map for a general multivector \\( A \\) is defined via its power series, but explicit closed forms are only available for certain classes of elements (e.g., blades or bivectors). For the purposes of this Laplace transform, we restrict to scalar \\( s \\) so that \\( e^{-st} \\) is well-defined and the integral is unambiguous.\n\nA key theoretical challenge in extending the Laplace transform to GA-valued functions is the non-commutativity of multivector multiplication. For example, if \\( s \\) were a general multivector, the ordering of \\( e^{-st} \\) and \\( f(t) \\) in the integrand would affect the result, and the exponential itself may not commute with \\( f(t) \\). By restricting \\( s \\) to scalars, we circumvent this issue and retain the essential properties of the classical Laplace transform, such as linearity and the ability to solve linear ODEs with GA-valued coefficients. As a concrete illustration, consider the GA-valued ODE \\( \\dot{f}(t) + a f(t) = 0 \\), where \\( a \\) is a fixed multivector. Applying our Laplace transform yields \\( s \\mathcal{L}[f](s) + a \\mathcal{L}[f](s) = f(0) \\), which can be solved for \\( \\mathcal{L}[f](s) \\) provided \\( s + a \\) is invertible in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\).\n\nOur approach is applicable to geometric algebras with signature \\( p+q \\leq 5 \\). This restriction is motivated by both computational and theoretical considerations. In higher-dimensional algebras, the explicit computation of the exponential map for general multivectors becomes intractable due to the exponential growth in the number of basis elements and the lack of closed-form expressions for the exponential of arbitrary multivectors (see Ablamowicz & Fauser, 2004). For example, in \\(\\mathcal{G}(5,0)\\), the algebra has 32 basis elements, and the exponential of a general multivector involves a sum over all grades, making symbolic computation and practical application extremely challenging. By focusing on low-dimensional cases, we ensure that the Laplace transform and its properties can be explicitly computed and applied to practical problems in engineering and physics.\n\nTo demonstrate the utility of our approach, consider a simple RLC circuit where the voltage and current are represented as multivectors in \\(\\mathcal{G}(3,0)\\), encoding both magnitude and orientation. The circuit equation can be written as \\( L \\dot{I}(t) + R I(t) + \\frac{1}{C} \\int I(t) dt = V(t) \\), where \\( I(t) \\) and \\( V(t) \\) are multivector-valued functions. Applying our Laplace transform yields \\( (L s + R + \\frac{1}{C s}) \\mathcal{L}[I](s) = \\mathcal{L}[V](s) \\), mirroring the classical result but now valid for multivector-valued signals. This enables the direct application of GA-based modeling to circuit analysis, preserving geometric information throughout the computation.\n\nIn summary, the objectives of this work are: (1) to motivate and demonstrate the use of GA in the analysis of dynamical systems and electrical circuits, as illustrated by the RLC circuit example; (2) to provide a rigorous and explicit definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in GA, clarifying the role of the scalar parameter and the exponential map; and (3) to delineate the scope and limitations of this extension, including the restriction to low-dimensional algebras and the challenges of generalizing to higher dimensions. Our results lay the groundwork for further applications of GA-based methods in system theory and circuit analysis, and highlight open questions regarding the extension of these techniques to higher-dimensional algebras and non-scalar Laplace parameters."}}